00:11:03,501 --> 00:11:05,202
[SPEAKER_01]: I'd say they're both about the same way.

194
00:11:05,222 --> 00:11:06,423
[SPEAKER_01]: Both big men.

195
00:11:07,664 --> 00:11:07,944
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

196
00:11:07,964 --> 00:11:15,569
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you ever have any umpire problems, that is, with called pitches?

197
00:11:16,069 --> 00:11:17,510
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I didn't.

198
00:11:18,791 --> 00:11:20,532
[SPEAKER_01]: I never had no trouble with umpires.

199
00:11:21,172 --> 00:11:27,216
[SPEAKER_01]: The only umpire I ever had trouble with was in Newark, New Jersey one night when I was in an international league.

200
00:11:28,399 --> 00:11:30,801
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll tell you who was umpiring.

201
00:11:30,821 --> 00:11:32,883
[SPEAKER_01]: Remember Cal Hubbard, the old football player?

202
00:11:33,924 --> 00:11:38,288
[SPEAKER_01]: Used to play the Detroit Lions, and then he went to, became an umpire in the American League.

203
00:11:38,308 --> 00:11:40,990
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, he was over there umpiring, that was before he went up to the American League.

204
00:11:42,511 --> 00:11:50,758
[SPEAKER_01]: And he was umpiring back the plate this night, and I thought I was getting strikes, see, and I rushed in a couple times, and Hubbard, God, get back in the mound.

205
00:11:50,839 --> 00:11:55,843
[SPEAKER_01]: And he looked, if you looked at Hubbard, he looked with all that paraphernalia, he looked about that wide, you know.

206
00:11:57,656 --> 00:12:02,517
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I don't know, it was late in the game, he called one and I ran off the mound.

207
00:12:02,617 --> 00:12:04,638
[SPEAKER_01]: I, you, said, I'm blind.

208
00:12:04,958 --> 00:12:06,858
[SPEAKER_01]: And boy, he walked out behind the catcher.

209
00:12:06,878 --> 00:12:10,339
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, get back on that mound, you bush or I'll lock you back there.

210
00:12:10,419 --> 00:12:13,700
[SPEAKER_01]: He looked, he looked like a big tank walking out there.

211
00:12:14,360 --> 00:12:16,321
[SPEAKER_01]: So I turned around, started backing around.

212
00:12:16,381 --> 00:12:18,181
[SPEAKER_01]: I still was cursing him, see.

213
00:12:19,101 --> 00:12:19,901
[SPEAKER_01]: And he was following me.

214
00:12:19,921 --> 00:12:20,842
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you say, kid?

215
00:12:21,262 --> 00:12:23,762
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, I ain't, I'm not even talking to you.

216
00:12:25,443 --> 00:12:26,383
[SPEAKER_01]: He's a big man.

217
00:12:26,971 --> 00:12:29,412
[SPEAKER_01]: I wouldn't want to tangle with him.

218
00:12:31,233 --> 00:12:33,635
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I never had any trouble with the umpires.

219
00:12:33,655 --> 00:12:34,875
[SPEAKER_02]: I think they're pretty good.

220
00:12:35,656 --> 00:12:40,239
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I think umpires, that's a tough job.

221
00:12:40,639 --> 00:12:46,442
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, it's so easy to be wrong, you know what I mean?

222
00:12:47,883 --> 00:12:51,525
[SPEAKER_01]: So I think they do a terrific job.

223
00:12:51,565 --> 00:12:53,946
[SPEAKER_01]: The umpire I got the kick out of was old Clem.

224
00:12:53,966 --> 00:12:55,327
[SPEAKER_01]: Remember him, old Bill Clem?

225
00:12:56,729 --> 00:13:04,390
[SPEAKER_01]: And you didn't argue with Bill Clem, because he had his hand up, but if you even started to open your mouth before he said anything, you were out.

226
00:13:04,630 --> 00:13:05,350
[SPEAKER_01]: You know what I mean?

227
00:13:05,951 --> 00:13:08,031
[SPEAKER_02]: What was it, some word you called him?

228
00:13:08,051 --> 00:13:08,571
[SPEAKER_02]: Catfish.

229
00:13:08,591 --> 00:13:09,811
[SPEAKER_01]: Catfish.

230
00:13:10,231 --> 00:13:12,092
[SPEAKER_01]: Hey, he'd clean the bench on that word.

231
00:13:13,372 --> 00:13:15,472
[SPEAKER_01]: We had a pitcher by the name of Barrett, Red Barrett.

232
00:13:15,492 --> 00:13:17,272
[SPEAKER_01]: He just came to us from Cincinnati.

233
00:13:18,613 --> 00:13:20,893
[SPEAKER_01]: And he was riding old Clem one day.

234
00:13:20,913 --> 00:13:23,033
[SPEAKER_01]: We told him one day, I said, get on that umpire.

235
00:13:23,073 --> 00:13:25,134
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, boy, Clem was back to the plate.

236
00:13:25,154 --> 00:13:26,054
[SPEAKER_01]: So Red was on him.

237
00:13:26,780 --> 00:13:27,861
[SPEAKER_01]: Clem never looked around.

238
00:13:27,881 --> 00:13:30,002
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, I know your voice, Mr. Barrett.

239
00:13:30,022 --> 00:13:32,724
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, you better keep quiet or out you go.

240
00:13:33,425 --> 00:13:35,386
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, Barrett kept quiet for a while.

241
00:13:35,426 --> 00:13:36,787
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, call him Catfish.

242
00:13:37,728 --> 00:13:38,849
[SPEAKER_01]: He called him Catfish.

243
00:13:38,909 --> 00:13:40,530
[SPEAKER_01]: And boy, that mask flew off.

244
00:13:40,550 --> 00:13:42,331
[SPEAKER_01]: And he come over, and he just cleaned the bench.

245
00:13:43,512 --> 00:13:45,894
[SPEAKER_01]: He didn't, where he got that name, I don't know.

246
00:13:45,934 --> 00:13:47,535
[SPEAKER_02]: But Barrett, he let it stay in.

247
00:13:47,615 --> 00:13:47,935
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

248
00:13:48,116 --> 00:13:49,456
[SPEAKER_01]: No, he cleaned the whole bench.

249
00:13:49,717 --> 00:13:50,577
[SPEAKER_02]: Barrett was on it.

250
00:13:50,757 --> 00:13:52,138
[SPEAKER_01]: Barrett was on the bench, see.

251
00:13:53,860 --> 00:13:54,220
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

252
00:13:55,177 --> 00:13:56,597
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, he didn't like that.

253
00:13:56,637 --> 00:13:57,898
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, I don't know where or what.

254
00:13:58,198 --> 00:14:00,378
[SPEAKER_01]: I've heard that story, but I've never heard why.

255
00:14:02,059 --> 00:14:09,140
[SPEAKER_01]: And with Dean, you know, when Diz would charge him, Clem used to walk up and draw a line with his toe and say, cross over that line, Diz.

256
00:14:09,520 --> 00:14:12,541
[SPEAKER_01]: And Diz would get up to it, but he never got over it.

257
00:14:12,621 --> 00:14:14,002
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you know him very well?

258
00:14:14,302 --> 00:14:16,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I knew Diz pretty well, yeah.

259
00:14:17,142 --> 00:14:20,003
[SPEAKER_01]: He was quite a guy and a great pitcher.

260
00:14:20,483 --> 00:14:21,083
[SPEAKER_01]: Great pitcher.

261
00:14:21,143 --> 00:14:21,943
[SPEAKER_01]: Terrific pitcher.

262
00:14:24,838 --> 00:14:38,669
[SPEAKER_01]: I, in fact, I picked a team the other day for, I don't know, some fella sent me a thing to pick an all-star, all-time all-star team, and I picked Hubble as, I picked a left- and right-hand pitcher.

263
00:14:38,749 --> 00:14:42,412
[SPEAKER_01]: I picked Hubble, Kyle Hubble, as a left-hander.

264
00:14:43,152 --> 00:14:46,955
[SPEAKER_01]: And it was debating between Dean or Fowler.

265
00:14:47,095 --> 00:14:48,156
[SPEAKER_01]: I finally picked Fowler.

266
00:14:49,228 --> 00:14:50,629
[SPEAKER_01]: But you could have went either way there.

267
00:14:50,689 --> 00:14:52,690
[SPEAKER_01]: But I was going by Feller's record.

268
00:14:52,731 --> 00:14:57,754
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, you have to almost pick him as one of the greatest of all time.

269
00:14:58,294 --> 00:14:58,715
[SPEAKER_01]: I would.

270
00:14:59,735 --> 00:15:01,177
[SPEAKER_02]: Of course, I grew up in Cleveland.

271
00:15:01,197 --> 00:15:02,357
[SPEAKER_02]: That was my team.

272
00:15:02,618 --> 00:15:05,580
[SPEAKER_02]: And I saw Feller pitch a lot.

273
00:15:06,300 --> 00:15:09,522
[SPEAKER_02]: I always wondered why his earned run average was always so high.

274
00:15:11,304 --> 00:15:12,505
[SPEAKER_02]: He was such a great pitcher.

275
00:15:12,925 --> 00:15:14,666
[SPEAKER_02]: He didn't have a lower ERA.

276
00:15:14,866 --> 00:15:15,627
[SPEAKER_01]: What was his ERA?

277
00:15:15,647 --> 00:15:15,927
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know.

278
00:15:15,947 --> 00:15:16,688
[SPEAKER_02]: I think it was over 300.

279
00:15:17,717 --> 00:15:18,478
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that right?

280
00:15:18,658 --> 00:15:19,559
[SPEAKER_02]: I'd have to check now.

281
00:15:19,579 --> 00:15:20,720
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't think he ever was under...

282
00:15:20,820 --> 00:15:22,782
[SPEAKER_01]: I would have never thought that would have been that high.

283
00:15:22,822 --> 00:15:24,243
[SPEAKER_02]: Even his great years he was over.

284
00:15:24,263 --> 00:15:26,465
[SPEAKER_02]: Of course, ERAs were higher then, generally.

285
00:15:26,565 --> 00:15:31,890
[SPEAKER_02]: Of course, he won a lot of ballgames.

286
00:15:31,910 --> 00:15:33,852
[SPEAKER_02]: He would have won a lot more had it not been for the war.

287
00:15:35,033 --> 00:15:39,157
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, maybe it's because he pitched a lot of low hit games.

288
00:15:39,947 --> 00:15:46,952
[SPEAKER_01]: But he walked a lot, too, you know, and when you walk maybe two in an inning, somebody bangs one, there's a run or two coming in, and I don't know.

289
00:15:47,052 --> 00:15:48,953
[SPEAKER_02]: I always thought he was inconsistent.

290
00:15:49,093 --> 00:15:50,454
[SPEAKER_02]: He lost a lot of games, too.

291
00:15:50,474 --> 00:15:51,695
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, he lost.

292
00:15:51,755 --> 00:15:52,696
[SPEAKER_02]: 127, lose 11.

293
00:15:52,716 --> 00:15:53,056
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

294
00:15:54,557 --> 00:15:58,800
[SPEAKER_01]: But I was just going, I didn't know whether to pick Dean or pick him.

295
00:15:59,680 --> 00:16:01,281
[SPEAKER_01]: I think in any... Can't go wrong.

296
00:16:01,361 --> 00:16:02,142
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I...

297
00:16:02,888 --> 00:16:05,369
[SPEAKER_01]: Then I picked Hubble over Grove and some of them.

298
00:16:05,449 --> 00:16:07,369
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, maybe I shouldn't.

299
00:16:07,409 --> 00:16:09,330
[SPEAKER_02]: I interviewed Lefty Grove last year.

300
00:16:09,710 --> 00:16:11,950
[SPEAKER_02]: I just got him in time.

301
00:16:12,391 --> 00:16:14,271
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, was he sick then or what?

302
00:16:14,391 --> 00:16:15,311
[SPEAKER_01]: No, he looked great.

303
00:16:15,672 --> 00:16:16,152
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that right?

304
00:16:16,172 --> 00:16:18,512
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, Ruffing looked great the last time I saw him.

305
00:16:18,712 --> 00:16:21,633
[SPEAKER_01]: But my God, that day at New Pemberstown, I seen him, I couldn't believe it.

306
00:16:22,253 --> 00:16:27,795
[SPEAKER_01]: See, I talked to him up in New Philly here just a couple of years before, a couple of years ago.

307
00:16:28,935 --> 00:16:30,697
[SPEAKER_01]: And I made the remark to some of the fellas.

308
00:16:30,757 --> 00:16:33,139
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, boy, Red looks like he could go nine now.

309
00:16:33,159 --> 00:16:34,600
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, he looks good, you know.

310
00:16:35,621 --> 00:16:41,385
[SPEAKER_02]: And he's had two heart attacks, two strokes, lost a kidney, I think.

311
00:16:41,966 --> 00:16:42,847
[SPEAKER_02]: I'll be darned.

312
00:16:43,327 --> 00:16:44,128
[SPEAKER_02]: We interviewed him.

313
00:16:44,228 --> 00:16:46,290
[SPEAKER_02]: I spent an hour or so at his home.

314
00:16:46,710 --> 00:16:47,551
[SPEAKER_02]: Very good.

315
00:16:47,591 --> 00:16:48,491
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, he could talk.

316
00:16:48,531 --> 00:16:48,912
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

317
00:16:49,833 --> 00:16:52,435
[SPEAKER_02]: And his right arm was used.

318
00:16:52,635 --> 00:16:55,537
[SPEAKER_02]: His left arm was totally useless, as we saw there.

319
00:16:55,757 --> 00:16:56,158
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

320
00:17:06,546 --> 00:17:08,748
[SPEAKER_02]: Tell me, did you ever hit any batters in the head?

321
00:17:09,308 --> 00:17:11,009
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I hit several.

322
00:17:11,869 --> 00:17:15,732
[SPEAKER_02]: Did that shake you up at all, or how did you react to that?

323
00:17:15,952 --> 00:17:18,313
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I've been hit a couple times myself.

324
00:17:19,874 --> 00:17:25,517
[SPEAKER_01]: The one that I hit that I, well, I never hit anybody I threw at.

325
00:17:26,017 --> 00:17:27,358
[SPEAKER_01]: I threw at batters, yeah.

326
00:17:28,616 --> 00:17:36,160
[SPEAKER_01]: But the one that hit that upset me the most was happening in the spring training game coming north with Cincinnati.

327
00:17:36,180 --> 00:17:37,961
[SPEAKER_01]: We were with the Boston Red Sox.

328
00:17:39,642 --> 00:17:43,164
[SPEAKER_01]: We were playing at Duke University at Durham, an exhibition game.

329
00:17:44,525 --> 00:17:47,106
[SPEAKER_01]: And you remember Mel Almeida?

330
00:17:47,446 --> 00:17:47,646
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

331
00:17:47,926 --> 00:17:48,907
[SPEAKER_01]: Was with the Red Sox.

332
00:17:48,947 --> 00:17:54,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Supposedly one of the best looking young outfielders that had come up in a long time.

333
00:17:55,090 --> 00:17:56,171
[SPEAKER_01]: He was a left-hand hitter.

334
00:17:56,845 --> 00:18:01,828
[SPEAKER_01]: But he had a habit, if you pitched him up in here, when he took that ball, he'd drop his head.

335
00:18:02,088 --> 00:18:02,669
[SPEAKER_01]: You know what I mean?

336
00:18:02,689 --> 00:18:05,571
[SPEAKER_01]: He wouldn't pull back or move back from the plate.

337
00:18:05,591 --> 00:18:06,471
[SPEAKER_01]: He'd drop his head.

338
00:18:07,832 --> 00:18:12,255
[SPEAKER_01]: And I was pitching that day in Durham, and he came up to hit.

339
00:18:14,296 --> 00:18:18,539
[SPEAKER_01]: And I threw a fastball tight up in here, and he dropped his head, and I hit him right there.

340
00:18:18,979 --> 00:18:20,320
[SPEAKER_01]: And that ball didn't bounce.

341
00:18:20,520 --> 00:18:23,762
[SPEAKER_01]: It just hit there and fell.

342
00:18:23,882 --> 00:18:26,144
[SPEAKER_01]: And because I walked in home plate, he would land there.

343
00:18:26,993 --> 00:18:29,214
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, oh my god, I thought I killed him.

344
00:18:29,294 --> 00:18:32,636
[SPEAKER_01]: And I walked back to the mound.

345
00:18:32,896 --> 00:18:38,318
[SPEAKER_01]: And I remembered Boston had Bing Miller was coaching first.

346
00:18:38,338 --> 00:18:38,918
[SPEAKER_01]: Remember him?

347
00:18:39,839 --> 00:18:41,479
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he charged me.

348
00:18:41,599 --> 00:18:42,800
[SPEAKER_01]: He said I was throwing at him.

349
00:18:42,820 --> 00:18:45,161
[SPEAKER_01]: We got kind of a scuffle there in the mound.

350
00:18:46,061 --> 00:18:51,424
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, Grove and that bunch, they broke it up.

351
00:18:51,444 --> 00:18:53,885
[SPEAKER_01]: They said, oh, that kid, he ain't throwing at him.

352
00:18:55,111 --> 00:18:56,773
[SPEAKER_01]: And they knew that to have it, he had to.

353
00:18:56,793 --> 00:19:02,037
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, he was in the hospital down at Duke University for I don't know how long, quite a while.

354
00:19:03,279 --> 00:19:05,641
[SPEAKER_01]: And he came back to Boston, but he was gun-shy.

355
00:19:05,781 --> 00:19:07,322
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, I talked to him.

356
00:19:08,023 --> 00:19:09,504
[SPEAKER_01]: He knew I wasn't throwing at him.

357
00:19:10,805 --> 00:19:15,770
[SPEAKER_01]: But he told me that he felt good up there, but he just couldn't stay up there anymore.

358
00:19:15,910 --> 00:19:18,533
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, he was... Well, then he came over to Brooklyn.

359
00:19:18,553 --> 00:19:21,015
[SPEAKER_01]: Then he went around to two or three clubs and drifted out.

360
00:19:21,992 --> 00:19:25,453
[SPEAKER_01]: And I always, in my heart, felt real, real sorry for him.

361
00:19:26,193 --> 00:19:27,474
[SPEAKER_01]: But it was unintentional.

362
00:19:27,514 --> 00:19:28,574
[SPEAKER_01]: It wasn't intentional.

363
00:19:28,634 --> 00:19:31,455
[SPEAKER_01]: And I don't know.

364
00:19:33,076 --> 00:19:35,436
[SPEAKER_01]: Something like that, you know, kind of hurts you pretty deep.

365
00:19:35,636 --> 00:19:37,497
[SPEAKER_01]: I talked to Leroy Parmelee.

366
00:19:38,017 --> 00:19:38,877
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you know Parmelee?

367
00:19:39,058 --> 00:19:39,718
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah.

368
00:19:40,098 --> 00:19:41,878
[SPEAKER_02]: He hit five people in his career.

369
00:19:41,918 --> 00:19:42,879
[SPEAKER_02]: He was very wild.

370
00:19:43,039 --> 00:19:43,299
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

371
00:19:43,319 --> 00:19:44,199
[SPEAKER_02]: Five of them in the head.

372
00:19:44,879 --> 00:19:49,301
[SPEAKER_02]: And he says he just couldn't pitch anymore that day when he hit a guy.

373
00:19:49,321 --> 00:19:49,821
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, yeah.

374
00:19:51,022 --> 00:19:53,024
[SPEAKER_01]: It don't make you feel good, no.

375
00:19:53,104 --> 00:19:54,425
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not feeling very good.

376
00:19:54,565 --> 00:19:56,546
[SPEAKER_01]: I know I, they took me out right away.

377
00:19:57,747 --> 00:20:01,189
[SPEAKER_01]: I left the ball game right away, and I was glad I did.

378
00:20:02,450 --> 00:20:05,412
[SPEAKER_01]: And I checked out the hospital, we were there the next day yet.

379
00:20:05,612 --> 00:20:10,135
[SPEAKER_01]: I couldn't get in to see him, but I'm, you know, and I called several times after we left there.

380
00:20:12,036 --> 00:20:13,457
[SPEAKER_01]: But I was worried, plenty worried.

381
00:20:14,017 --> 00:20:15,718
[SPEAKER_01]: But I, cause I knew I hit him hard.

382
00:20:16,399 --> 00:20:18,440
[SPEAKER_01]: Cause when you hit a fellow and the ball glances,

383
00:20:19,442 --> 00:20:27,386
[SPEAKER_01]: But when a ball hits there and then just drops, you know you're hitting, it's hitting pretty hard.

384
00:20:29,927 --> 00:20:36,711
[SPEAKER_01]: I know the day I got hit, one time, the time I was telling you about in Manassas, I knew the ball hit me.

385
00:20:37,731 --> 00:20:43,094
[SPEAKER_01]: But when it hit me, it was just like a ball of fire went down my spine into my backbone here.

386
00:20:44,134 --> 00:20:47,016
[SPEAKER_01]: Just like, I don't know, just, then I went out.

387
00:20:47,376 --> 00:20:48,717
[SPEAKER_01]: But I knew it, I felt that.

388
00:20:49,817 --> 00:20:51,459
[SPEAKER_01]: And I knew it was a bull when it hit me.

389
00:20:52,740 --> 00:20:58,948
[SPEAKER_01]: And I was trying to guess that picture because I'd been throwing it to him and I figured he was going to throw it to me.

390
00:21:00,411 --> 00:21:03,872
[SPEAKER_01]: But then this catcher, when I came up, said something to me.

391
00:21:03,992 --> 00:21:06,432
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, you better watch so and so.

392
00:21:06,452 --> 00:21:07,873
[SPEAKER_01]: You're going to get your head knocked off.

